"comparison": "Both companies disclose that credit rating downgrades can trigger requirements to post additional collateral or terminate agreements under bilateral derivative contracts. BAC states that counterparties 'may require' additional collateral or termination which could 'cause us to sustain losses', while Goldman Sachs provides a more specific operational detail, noting that it manages its Global Capital and Liquidity Adequacy (GCLA) specifically to ensure it can make the additional collateral or termination payments required 'in the event of a two-notch reduction' in its long-term credit ratings.",
